---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LiveFrame from '../components/LiveFrame.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';

const APP = 'https://alucify-screens.fly.dev';
const FIGMA =
  'https://www.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1';
const FIGMA_EMBED =
  'https://embed.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1&embed-host=share';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="A tool for reviewing code an AI agent wrote"
  metaTitle="Alucify — designing for AI agents working on a codebase | Tyler Young"
  description="A code-intelligence tool whose primary metrics are blast radius, agent hours and token cost — designed for people accountable for work an agent did."
  eyebrow="01 · AI-Native Product"
  meta={[
    ['Product', 'Code intelligence — “your code, flows, and intents, unified”'],
    ['Contribution', 'All product design — every surface, every state'],
    ['Surface', 'First run through tenured use · 20 documented states'],
    ['Status', 'Active venture · shown with the founder’s agreement'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      An AI agent writes the code. Someone still has to sign off on it. They did
      not watch it get written, so they need to know what it touched, how much work
      it was, and what it cost. I designed the tool that tells them.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <LiveFrame
    src={`${APP}/#/dashboard`}
    host="alucify-screens.fly.dev"
    title="The Alucify dashboard, running"
    note="Running now. Blast radius, agent hours and token cost are summed from the five concepts on the impact screen."
  />

  <h2>The three numbers on the landing screen</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Blast radius, agent hours, token cost. Not velocity, not lines written. You
      see how far the change reached, how much work it was and what it cost before
      you open anything.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Cost shows up again on each individual change, next to what it paid for. Most
      tools bury it in a billing page, where it never affects a decision.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Coverage, broken into decisions</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Under the percentage is every decision behind it. Each one is named,
      categorised as scope, architecture or data, and marked covered, partial or
      none. Worst first, so the gaps are the first thing you see.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <LiveFrame
    src={`${APP}/#/coverage`}
    host="alucify-screens.fly.dev"
    title="Coverage analysis, running"
    note="Running now. The score is derived from the 27 decisions below it, and the table sorts worst-first."
  />

  <h2>How severity is shown</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      This is the one place the product raises its voice. A change marked critical
      gets a summary and a breakdown across blast radius, regression risk, data
      integrity and backward compatibility. Everything else stays quiet so this
      reads as urgent.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <LiveFrame
    src={`${APP}/#/impact-detail`}
    host="alucify-screens.fly.dev"
    title="Impact analysis concept detail, running"
    note="Running now. Click a concept name in the table to open it, or use prev and next inside the panel."
  />

  <h2>Empty, loading and stale states</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      I designed twenty screens. Many are the ones usually left to engineering to
      improvise: an integration still running, an audit gone stale, a dashboard
      with nothing in it yet. People spend most of their time in these states, not
      in the full one.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="pair">
    <LiveFrame
      src={`${APP}/#/artifact-reaudit`}
      host="alucify-screens.fly.dev"
      title="An integrated artifact with a deprecated audit, running"
      note="Running now. Integrated, and the audit behind it has gone stale."
    />
    <LiveFrame
      src={`${APP}/#/in-progress`}
      host="alucify-screens.fly.dev"
      title="Integration in progress, running"
      note="Running now. The state the product spends real time in."
    />
  </div>

  <h2>All twenty screens</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Four flows. Getting set up runs from signing in to a context the product can
      reason about. The other three all start on the same dashboard, because that
      is the hub — artifacts, coverage and impact are the three ways out of it.
      Open the file to zoom into any of them.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  {/* Figma's embed, so a reader can pan and zoom the twenty frames without
      leaving the page. It needs the file shared as "anyone with the link can
      view"; the link underneath is what they fall back to if it is not. */}
  <figure class="figma">
    <iframe
      src={FIGMA_EMBED}
      title="The Alucify design file — twenty frames across four flows"
      loading="lazy"
      referrerpolicy="no-referrer"
      allowfullscreen
    ></iframe>
    <figcaption>
      20 frames · 4 flows ·{' '}
      <a href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        open in Figma <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What I owned</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      All of the design. Every screen and every state — the metrics on the landing
      screen, the coverage model, the severity system, and the twenty states from
      first run to daily use.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Shown with the founder's agreement. What is running above is the design
      rebuilt as a working app, not the shipping product — I built it so these
      screens could be opened rather than looked at. The tenant, the people and
      every figure in it are invented; the numbers reconcile because they are
      computed from the rows beneath them.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 36px !important;
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    gap: 20px;
    margin-bottom: 34px;
  }

  .pair :global(.shot) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
